Q: Y/d/m formatted date giving wrong day of week in PHP A: <p>Try this:</p>

<pre><code>date_default_timezone_set('Asia/Kolkata');
$date = '2017/06/07';
$date = str_replace('/','-', $date);
$weekday = date('l', strtotime($date)); 
echo $weekday;
// Output: Wednesday
</code></pre>

<p><a href="http://sandbox.onlinephpfunctions.com/code/f6570aac5183f24038841f24907637d41072b839" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Phpfiddle link</a></p>

<p><a href="http://php.net/manual/en/function.strtotime.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Check the document here</a></p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Note: Dates in the m/d/y or d-m-y formats are disambiguated by looking
  at the separator between the various components: if the separator is a
  slash (/), then the American m/d/y is assumed; whereas if the
  separator is a dash (-) or a dot (.), then the European d-m-y format
  is assumed. If, however, the year is given in a two digit format and
  the separator is a dash (-, the date string is parsed as y-m-d. To
  avoid potential ambiguity, it's best to use ISO 8601 (YYYY-MM-DD)
  dates or DateTime::createFromFormat() when possible.</p>
</blockquote>

Q: How to convert google.protobuf.Struct field in java.util.Map&lt;String, Object&gt;? A: <p>You can use the <code>toMap</code> function below to convert <code>protobuf.Struct</code> to <code>Map&lt;String, Object&gt;</code>.</p>
<pre><code>public static Object toObject(Value value) {
  return switch (value.getKindCase()) {
    case NULL_VALUE -&gt; null;
    case NUMBER_VALUE -&gt; value.getNumberValue();
    case STRING_VALUE -&gt; value.getStringValue();
    case BOOL_VALUE -&gt; value.getBoolValue();
    case LIST_VALUE -&gt; value.getListValue().getValuesList().stream().map((v) -&gt; toObject(v));
    case STRUCT_VALUE -&gt; toMap(value.getStructValue());
    default -&gt; throw new DomainException(DomainExceptionCode.VALUE_KIND_NOT_SUPPORTED);
  };
}

public static Map&lt;String, Object&gt; toMap(Struct struct) {
  Map&lt;String, Object&gt; map = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();

  struct.getFieldsMap().forEach((key, value) -&gt; {
    map.put(key, toObject(value));
  });

  return map;
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to get concrete Controller type using Guava A: <blockquote>
<p>Is there a different approach recommended?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I would recommend letting the controller take care of the initialization, providing it with the data it needs to do so, instead of calling a method to perform the initialization externally (as you are attempting to do). To this end, you can refactor like this:</p>
<p>First, give the controller access to the event via a constructor, and then just perform the initialization in the usual <code>initialize()</code> method:</p>
<pre><code>public class ContentOneController {

    private ChangeViewEventDataModel event ;

    public ContentOneController(ChangeViewEventDataModel event) {
        this.event = event;
    }

    public ContentOneController() {
        this(null);
    }

    @FXML
    private void initialize() {
        if (event != null) {
            setLabelText(event.getNewData());
        }
    }

    // exisiting code ...
}
</code></pre>
<p>Now you just need to modify the controller factory to have access to the event:</p>
<pre><code>public class ControllerFactory implements Callback&lt;Class&lt;?&gt;, Object&gt; {

    private ChangeViewEventDataModel event ;

    public ControllerFactory(ChangeViewEventDataModel event) {
        this.event = event;
    }


    @Override
    public Object call(Class&lt;?&gt; controllerType) {
        if (controllerType == ContentController.class) return new ContentController();
        if (controllerType == ContentHomeController.class) return new ContentHomeController();
        if (controllerType == ContentOneController.class) return new ContentOneController(event);
        throw new RuntimeException(&quot;Controller of type '&quot; + controllerType.getSimpleName() + &quot;' is not supported!&quot;);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>And finally just pass the event to the controller factory:</p>
<pre><code>@Subscribe
public void handleChangeViewDataModel(ChangeViewEventDataModel event) {
    try {

        FXMLLoader loader = new FXMLLoader(Objects.requireNonNull(getClass().getResource(event.getViewName())));
        loader.setControllerFactory(new ControllerFactory(event));
        Parent root = loader.load();

        // change the content pane
        contentController.getContent().getChildren().clear();
        contentController.getContent().getChildren().add(root);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        // ...
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Now the controller takes care of its own initialization, and you don't need to invoke the <code>setLabelText(...)</code> method externally. This would be my preferred approach, because the initialization happens in the &quot;natural&quot; place.</p>
<hr />
<p>If you prefer not to perform the refactoring, you can just use a switch with pattern matching (or even just a series of <code>if</code> statements using <code>instanceof</code>). This seems less preferable, because it involves a second round of testing for the type of controller, beyond that you already need to implement in the controller factory:</p>
<pre><code>@Subscribe
public void handleChangeViewDataModel(ChangeViewEventDataModel event) {
    try {

        FXMLLoader loader = new FXMLLoader(Objects.requireNonNull(getClass().getResource(event.getViewName())));
        loader.setControllerFactory(new ControllerFactory(event));
        Parent root = loader.load();

        // change the content pane
        contentController.getContent().getChildren().clear();
        contentController.getContent().getChildren().add(root);

        switch (loader.getController()) {
            case ContentOneController c1c -&gt; c1c.setLabelText(event.getNewData());
            // other cases as needed
            default -&gt; {}
        }

    } catch (Exception e) {
        // ...
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Object Look-up in JS Doesn&#39;t Work with WebGL2RenderingContext API Constants A: <p>Version #1 is simply wrong Javascript syntax. In an <code>ObjectLiteral</code> the only accepted <code>LiteralPropertyName</code>s are:  <code>IdentifierName</code>, <code>StringLiteral</code> and <code>NumericLiteral</code>, as given by the <a href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/#prod-LiteralPropertyName" rel="nofollow noreferrer">specs</a>.
<code>WebGL2RenderingContext.BYTE</code> is neither an indentifier (because of the dot), nor a string, nor a number.</p>
<p>Instead of a literal property name, one may use a computed property name, which is syntactically distinguished by its contents being included in square brackets, see the <a href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/#prod-ComputedPropertyName" rel="nofollow noreferrer">specs</a>. That's what you do in Version #2.</p>
<p>In this case, the expression in the square brackets is evaluated at runtime and the key will be its value if it is a a Symbol or a String, otherwise the standard conversion of that property to a string.</p>
<p>The values of the constants you use as property names in your object are numbers, as given on <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebGL_API/Constants#data_types" rel="nofollow noreferrer">MDN</a>, or from the source code where you extracted them from, for instance <code>WebGL2RenderingContext.BYTE</code> is <code>0x1400</code>, that is <code>5120</code>, so your object from Version #2 will be:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const WebGlDataTypes = {
   &quot;5120&quot;: &quot;BYTE&quot;
   &quot;5121&quot;: &quot;UNSIGNED_BYTE&quot;,
   .......
};
</code></pre>
